.TH TMUX-COPY-LAST-OUTPUT 1 "2026-10-12" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tmux-copy-last-output \- copy recent tmux command blocks to the clipboard
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tmux-copy-last-output
.RB [ \-\-print ]
.RB [ \-\-pane " PANE" ]
.RB [ \-\-client " CLIENT" ]
.RI [ count ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tmux-copy-last-output
captures the last 10,000 lines of the current tmux pane, detects shell
prompt boundaries, and copies the most recent command plus its output to the
clipboard. A command whose output is longer than that has scrolled its own
prompt out of range and is reported as not found. When
.I count
is supplied, it must be a positive base-10 integer and the command copies that
many recent command blocks. The default is one.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-print ,
the command writes the blocks to standard output instead of the clipboard, so
a caller can route them elsewhere, such as pasting them into another pane.
.PP
The parser recognizes only a two-line prompt. The first line starts with
.BR user@host: ,
optionally preceded by an exit code such as
.BR "[0] " ;
the user name may contain only letters, digits, and underscores. The line
directly below it is
.B $
or
.BR % ,
optionally followed by a space and the command, for example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
[0] user@host:~/src/project (main)
$ make test
.fi
.RE
.PP
These bash and zsh prompt settings produce that shape:
.PP
.RS
.nf
PS1='[$?] \eu@\eh:\ew\en$ '
PROMPT=$'[%?] %n@%m:%~\en%% '
.fi
.RE
.PP
If no complete command block is found, the command displays a
tmux message and exits successfully so keybindings do not fail noisily. With
.BR \-\-print
it exits with status 3 instead, so a pipeline routes nothing.
.SH CLIPBOARD BACKENDS
When installed,
.B clip copy
owns clipboard transport and private history. Pass the exact initiating
client name and pane ID from a tmux binding; this delivers OSC 52 through
.B tmux load-buffer -w -t CLIENT
targeting that name rather than the most recently active attachment or the
remote host's clipboard. Client names identify terminal devices; this API
does not pin a process generation across detach/reconnect and name reuse.
The pane ID also targets the capture explicitly.
.PP
Without clip, an explicit client still uses tmux's OSC 52 transport. Without
an explicit client the standalone command tries
.BR pbcopy (1)
on macOS,
.BR wl-copy (1)
when
.B WAYLAND_DISPLAY
is set, then
.BR xclip (1)
when
.B DISPLAY
is set. It never guesses an OSC 52 destination client. A missing initiating
client does not fall back to copying on the remote host.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command copied or printed output, reported that no block was found without
.BR \-\-print ,
or displayed a clipboard failure message through tmux.
.TP
.B 1
An unexpected shell, tmux, or parsing failure occurred before tmux messaging
could report the result.
.TP
.B 3
With
.BR \-\-print ,
no command block was found; the command reports that through tmux and prints
nothing.
.TP
.B 64
The arguments were invalid. The command writes a usage diagnostic before any
tmux or clipboard command is invoked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR term-notify-sound (1)
